# Local tests before GitHub push

Do not `git push`, `gh pr create`, or otherwise publish to GitHub until the **affected** Playwright tests have passed **three consecutive times** locally after the latest change.

Husky already blocks **commits** unless a single `npm run test:changed` pass succeeds (see `.husky/pre-commit`). The three-run gate below still applies before **push** / PR publish.

## Required sequence

Prefer Playwright’s change-aware selection (dependency graph over git diffs). Do **not** run the full suite by default.

```bash
# Dirty working tree (uncommitted edits)
npm run test:changed

# Already committed on a branch (compare to the base branch)
npm run test:changed:main
```

Before push, run whichever form matches the tree **three times**:

```bash
npm run test:changed:main
npm run test:changed:main
npm run test:changed:main
```

All three runs must exit 0. Keep the repo’s configured projects (do not drop browsers or use `--last-failed` as a substitute).

If `--only-changed` selects zero tests (e.g. docs-only / agent-prompt-only), that counts as a pass — do not force a full suite.

## When to run the full suite instead

Use `npx playwright test` (full suite, still three passes) only when the change can invalidate the dependency heuristic:

- `playwright.config.ts`, `package.json` / lockfile, or CI workflow changes
- Shared fixtures, global setup/teardown, or auth/`storageState` wiring
- You are unsure whether unrelated specs import the touched files

## Reset the count

- If any run fails, fix the failure, then start over at run 1.
- If you change specs, page objects, config, or fixtures after a passing streak, start over.

## Do not

```bash
# ❌ BAD — one green run, then push
npx playwright test --only-changed=main && git push

# ❌ BAD — drop browsers or reuse a failed-only rerun
npx playwright test --project=chromium
git push

# ❌ BAD — full suite every time when only a few files changed
npx playwright test
npx playwright test
npx playwright test
git push
```

```bash
# ✅ GOOD — three affected-test passes, then push
npm run test:changed:main
npm run test:changed:main
npm run test:changed:main
git push
```
